### Complete University Solutions: File Copying & Word Count with BufferedReader

#### 1. Architectural Benefit of Buffering:
Reading from physical storage character-by-character causes thousands of expensive OS disk read interrupts. `BufferedReader` pre-fetches blocks of characters (default 8 KB buffer) into memory, allowing `readLine()` to process tokens with maximum throughput.

#### 2. University Problem 1: Copy One File to Another
```java
import java.io.*;

public class FileCopyProgram {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        File source = new File("source.txt");
        File destination = new File("destination.txt");

        // Use try-with-resources for automatic file descriptor closing
        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(source));
             BufferedWriter writer = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter(destination))) {

            String line;
            int linesCopied = 0;

            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                writer.write(line);
                writer.newLine(); // Maintain cross-platform line breaks
                linesCopied++;
            }

            System.out.printf("File copy successful! Total lines copied: %d\n", linesCopied);

        } catch (FileNotFoundException fnf) {
            System.err.println("Error: Source file does not exist: " + fnf.getMessage());
        } catch (IOException ioe) {
            System.err.println("I/O Error during file copying: " + ioe.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

#### 3. University Problem 2: Count Words, Characters & Lines in a Text File
```java
import java.io.*;

public class WordCountInFile {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        File targetFile = new File("sample.txt");

        int lineCount = 0;
        int wordCount = 0;
        int charCount = 0;

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(targetFile))) {
            String currentLine;

            while ((currentLine = br.readLine()) != null) {
                lineCount++;
                charCount += currentLine.length();

                // Split line into words using whitespace regex
                String trimmed = currentLine.trim();
                if (!trimmed.isEmpty()) {
                    String[] words = trimmed.split("\\s+");
                    wordCount += words.length;
                }
            }

            System.out.println("=== File Statistics ===");
            System.out.println("File Name:        " + targetFile.getName());
            System.out.println("Total Lines:      " + lineCount);
            System.out.println("Total Words:      " + wordCount);
            System.out.println("Total Characters: " + charCount);

        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error reading file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{BufferedReader combined with FileReader provides high-speed, line-oriented text processing.}$$
